-- title:
--   Definitions for Linear programming with unitary-equivariant constraints
-- statement:
--   The objects the formalized statements of *Linear programming with unitary-equivariant constraints* (arXiv:2207.05713) are stated in. Each was checked against the paper by a blind read-back and a moderator.
--
--   - `UnitaryEquivariant`: Paper pp.9–10, Definition 2.1: covariance on every input density matrix.
--   - `SymmetricChannel`: Paper pp.11–12, Definition 2.3: independent input and output invariance.
--   - `contraction`: Paper pp.15–16, Eqs.(42),(51): the contraction between wires a and b.
--   - `physicalTower`: Paper p.24, Eq.(82): the physical matrix image at level k, embedded by identity factors on unused registers. Same-side swaps and cross-wall contractions are the physical images of the generators in pp.14–15.
--   - `centerSet`: Paper p.19, Eq.(65): the center of a concrete subalgebra, in ambient coordinates.
--   - `gtAlgebra`: Paper p.24, Eq.(83), and p.22, Definition 4.5: the GT algebra generated by all embedded centers up through level k.
--   - `jm`: Paper pp.25,27, Eqs.(85),(86): explicit lifted JM operator. The dual clause has priority when p=0, giving J_1=d I. The printed standalone J_1=0 is for an initial primal step; its overlap with the dual clause is corrected here.
--   - `AdditivelyCentral`: Paper p.22, Definition 4.7(a), including membership in the GT algebras.
--   - `Separating`: Paper p.22, Definition 4.7(b): generation of every GT level, including the membership condition in the definition's preamble.
--   - `YoungShape`: Paper p.9 and §5.1: reuse Mathlib's finite downwards closed Young diagrams. Zero-based cells have the same column-minus-row content as one-based cells.
--   - `shapeRows`: Paper p.9: the first-column length is the number of nonempty rows.
--   - `shapeContent`: Paper p.23, Eq.(76): total integer content, column minus row.
--   - `Bipartition`: Paper p.24, Eq.(84): left and right Young shapes.
--   - `AddsCell`: Paper p.24: one cell is added. This uses cell sets, independently of contents.
--   - `PhysicalPath`: Paper pp.24–26: an entire physical path, with inadmissible vertices excluded at every level. The root and each edge are constructed combinatorially.
--   - `pathContent`: Paper pp.25–28: the three edge contents, equivalently obtained by differencing total contents plus d times the right size (Eq.(93)); no projectors enter this definition.
--   - `Diagram`: Paper pp.13–15, Eq.(31): a walled diagram is encoded by its partially transposed permutation. This encoding includes the empty diagram.
--   - `flipDual`: Paper p.14, Eq.(31): exchange top and bottom endpoints on the dual side.
--   - `traceConnectivity`: Paper pp.15,57–58: connected components after gluing opposite endpoints in S. False denotes the top row; true denotes the bottom row.
--   - `relationMatrix`: Paper p.15, Eq.(43): every connected pair must have the same basis label.
--   - `diagramMatrix`: Paper p.15, Eqs.(41)–(43): physical action of a single walled diagram.
--   - `DiagramSum`: Paper pp.13,15: the vector space of formal complex diagram combinations; no multiplication on this vector space is needed for the trace statements.
--   - `diagramAction`: Paper p.15, Eq.(41), extended complex-linearly to all diagrams.
--   - `closedLoops`: Paper p.15, Eq.(38): closed components are exactly those containing no untraced endpoint. Count components of the finite graph, independently of matrix traces.
--   - `diagramTrace`: Paper p.15, Eq.(37): diagram trace is d to the number of closed loops, extended linearly, with empty closure value d^0=1.
--   - `Remaining`: Paper p.15, Eq.(38): remaining wires keep their original order on each side of the wall. Subtype indices avoid an arbitrary renumbering.
--   - `contractedRelation`: Paper p.15, Eq.(38): the contracted diagram is the connectivity relation induced on retained endpoints after gluing in S and erasing closed components.
--   - `diagramPartialTrace`: Paper p.15, Eq.(38): diagrammatic partial trace; relations encode the remaining matching, so parallel terms are combined as a formal linear combination.
--   - `relationAction`: Paper p.15, Eq.(43): action of a formal combination of retained diagram relations.
--   - `joinLabels`: Paper p.58, Eq.(188): assemble fixed retained labels and summed traced labels.
--   - `matrixPartialTrace`: Paper pp.57–58, Eq.(188): standard partial matrix trace on arbitrary subsets.
--   - `qubitEigenvalueDensity`: Paper pp.38–39, specialized to d=k=2: after sorting the two eigenvalues, the largest eigenvalue t has density 6(2t-1)^2 on [1/2,1]. This follows directly from the normalized density in Eq.(147), where k=d and no singular strata occur.
--   - `qubitSpectralState`: Paper p.38: the state with eigenvalues t,1-t and unknown eigenbasis U.
--   - `qubitThresholdSuccess`: Paper pp.38–39, Eqs.(150),(151), source-proof reading: one fixed effect P is chosen before the unknown U; the integral averages only the induced eigenvalue law. The strict lower branch and weak upper branch partition the threshold event.
--   - `qubitThresholdGuarantees`: Paper pp.38–39: achievable worst-case guarantees of a two-outcome POVM. This avoids taking a real infimum or supremum on an unchecked empty set.
--   - `firstBreakpointPolynomial`: Paper p.39: the polynomial defining the first breakpoint.
--   - `secondBreakpointPolynomial`: Paper p.39: the polynomial defining the second breakpoint; its x^2 coefficient is -45.
--   - `firstBreakpoint`: Paper p.39: isolate the first root rather than equating it to a rounded decimal. The theorem below establishes membership and uniqueness, so the empty-set default is excluded.
--   - `secondBreakpoint`: Paper p.39: isolate the second root; membership and uniqueness are part of the result.
--   - `threeCopySuccessCurve`: Paper p.39, Eq.(152), visually checked: the middle coefficient is 6/5.
--   - `oneCopySuccessCurve`: Paper p.39, Eq.(153): the one-copy lower bound.
--   - `frequency`: Paper pp.39–41, Eqs.(154),(156): frequency of one symbol in three inputs.
--   - `majoritySuccess`: Paper pp.40–41: fidelity uses the projector onto ALL most frequent symbols, without normalizing that projector to a mixture.
--   - `RealizableCloning`: Paper p.42, Eq.(159): physical asymmetric cloning region. All real shrinkage parameters, including negative ones, are permitted; the same channel works on every state.
--   - `qubitCloneCoordinates`: Paper p.42, Eqs.(162)–(164): the qubit affine map. A is the scalar block, B the size-two block, and C the size-three block; entries are zero-based.
--   - `qutritCloneCoordinates`: Paper p.44, Eqs.(167)–(169): qutrit affine coordinates. A,B,C,D correspond to the source's blocks X^1,X^2,X^3,X^4 with sizes 1,2,3,3.
--   - `FourUseProtocol`: Paper p.44, §7.4: a four-slot deterministic sequential protocol. A single finite memory dimension suffices by padding the intermediate memories. All five channels are part of the protocol, hence independent of the unknown U.
--   - `FourUseProtocol.run`: Paper p.44: four applications of the same unknown qubit unitary, on the query register only, with the identity on memory. No controlled oracle is supplied.
-- source:
--   Linear programming with unitary-equivariant constraints, arXiv:2207.05713v2, https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.05713v2

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Defs_Channel
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Defs_TensorPermutationRepresentations
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Defs_GradientMethods
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Information100_P69

/-!
Statements from Grinko and Ozols, arXiv:2207.05713v2.
Paper-specific new objects are local; shared objects use frozen imports.
Registers use zero-based wire labels;
mathematical JM index k corresponds to `k.val + 1`. Compilation is pending.
-/

namespace QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Claimed50.P02

open scoped BigOperators Matrix ComplexConjugate ComplexOrder

open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Information100.P69 (Sites choi)

noncomputable section

example {d : ℕ} (U : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℂ) (x y : (Sites (Fin d) 0)) :
    piKron 0 U x y = 1 := by simp [piKron]

example {d p q : ℕ} (Phi : Superop (Sites (Fin d) p) (Sites (Fin d) q))
    (a b : (Sites (Fin d) p)) (c e : (Sites (Fin d) q)) :
    choi Phi (a,c) (b,e) = Phi (Matrix.single a b 1) c e := by rfl

/-- Paper pp.9–10, Definition 2.1: covariance on every input density matrix. -/
def UnitaryEquivariant {d p q : ℕ}
    (Phi : Superop (Sites (Fin d) p) (Sites (Fin d) q)) : Prop :=
  ∀ U : Matrix.unitaryGroup (Fin d) ℂ, ∀ rho, IsDensityMatrix rho →
    Phi (conjSuperop (piKron p (U : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℂ)) rho) =
      conjSuperop (piKron q (U : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℂ)) (Phi rho)

/-- Paper pp.11–12, Definition 2.3: independent input and output invariance. -/
def SymmetricChannel {d p q : ℕ}
    (Phi : Superop (Sites (Fin d) p) (Sites (Fin d) q)) : Prop :=
  (∀ pi : Equiv.Perm (Fin p), ∀ rho, IsDensityMatrix rho →
    Phi (conjSuperop ((QAlgorithms.Schur.tensorPermutation _ _) pi) rho) = Phi rho) ∧
  (∀ sigma : Equiv.Perm (Fin q), ∀ rho, IsDensityMatrix rho →
    conjSuperop ((QAlgorithms.Schur.tensorPermutation _ _) sigma) (Phi rho) = Phi rho)

/-- Paper pp.15–16, Eqs.(42),(51): the contraction between wires a and b. -/
def contraction {d n : ℕ} (a b : Fin n) :
    Matrix (Sites (Fin d) n) (Sites (Fin d) n) ℂ := by
  classical
  exact fun x y => if x a = x b ∧ y a = y b ∧
    (∀ i, i ≠ a → i ≠ b → x i = y i) then 1 else 0

/-- Paper p.24, Eq.(82): the physical matrix image at level k, embedded by
identity factors on unused registers. Same-side swaps and cross-wall contractions
are the physical images of the generators in pp.14–15. -/
def physicalTower (d p q k : ℕ) :
    Subalgebra ℂ (Matrix (Sites (Fin d) (p+q)) (Sites (Fin d) (p+q)) ℂ) :=
  Algebra.adjoin ℂ {M | ∃ a b : Fin (p+q), a.val < k ∧ b.val < k ∧ a ≠ b ∧
    (((a.val < p ↔ b.val < p) ∧ M = (QAlgorithms.Schur.tensorPermutation _ _) (Equiv.swap a b)) ∨
      (a.val < p ∧ p ≤ b.val ∧ M = contraction a b))}

/-- Paper p.19, Eq.(65): the center of a concrete subalgebra, in ambient coordinates. -/
def centerSet {R : Type*} [Ring R] [Algebra ℂ R] (A : Subalgebra ℂ R) : Set R :=
  {x | x ∈ A ∧ ∀ y ∈ A, x * y = y * x}

/-- Paper p.24, Eq.(83), and p.22, Definition 4.5: the GT algebra generated by
all embedded centers up through level k. -/
def gtAlgebra (d p q k : ℕ) :
    Subalgebra ℂ (Matrix (Sites (Fin d) (p+q)) (Sites (Fin d) (p+q)) ℂ) :=
  Algebra.adjoin ℂ {M | ∃ j : ℕ, 1 ≤ j ∧ j ≤ k ∧ M ∈ centerSet (physicalTower d p q j)}

/-- Paper pp.25,27, Eqs.(85),(86): explicit lifted JM operator. The dual clause
has priority when p=0, giving J_1=d I. The printed standalone J_1=0 is for
an initial primal step; its overlap with the dual clause is corrected here. -/
def jm (d p q : ℕ) (k : Fin (p+q)) :
    Matrix (Sites (Fin d) (p+q)) (Sites (Fin d) (p+q)) ℂ := by
  classical
  exact if k.val < p then
    ∑ i : Fin (p+q), if i.val < k.val then (QAlgorithms.Schur.tensorPermutation _ _) (Equiv.swap i k) else 0
  else
    (∑ i : Fin (p+q), if p ≤ i.val ∧ i.val < k.val then
      (QAlgorithms.Schur.tensorPermutation _ _) (Equiv.swap i k) else 0) -
    (∑ i : Fin (p+q), if i.val < p then contraction i k else 0) + (d : ℂ) • 1

example (d : ℕ) : jm d 1 0 0 = 0 := by simp [jm]

example (d : ℕ) : jm d 0 1 0 = (d : ℂ) • 1 := by simp [jm]

/-- Paper p.22, Definition 4.7(a), including membership in the GT algebras. -/
def AdditivelyCentral (d p q : ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∀ k : Fin (p+q), jm d p q k ∈ gtAlgebra d p q (k.val+1) ∧
    (∑ i : Fin (p+q), if i.val ≤ k.val then jm d p q i else 0) ∈
      centerSet (physicalTower d p q (k.val+1))

/-- Paper p.22, Definition 4.7(b): generation of every GT level, including the
membership condition in the definition's preamble. -/
def Separating (d p q : ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∀ k : Fin (p+q), jm d p q k ∈ gtAlgebra d p q (k.val+1) ∧
    gtAlgebra d p q (k.val+1) =
      Algebra.adjoin ℂ {M | ∃ i : Fin (p+q), i.val ≤ k.val ∧ M = jm d p q i}

/-- Paper p.9 and §5.1: reuse Mathlib's finite downwards closed Young diagrams.
Zero-based cells have the same column-minus-row content as one-based cells. -/
abbrev YoungShape := YoungDiagram

/-- Paper p.9: the first-column length is the number of nonempty rows. -/
def shapeRows (s : YoungShape) : ℕ := s.colLen 0

/-- Paper p.23, Eq.(76): total integer content, column minus row. -/
def shapeContent (s : YoungShape) : ℤ :=
  ∑ a ∈ s.cells, ((a.2 : ℤ) - (a.1 : ℤ))

/-- Paper p.24, Eq.(84): left and right Young shapes. -/
abbrev Bipartition := YoungShape × YoungShape

/-- Paper p.24: one cell is added. This uses cell sets, independently of contents. -/
def AddsCell (s t : YoungShape) : Prop :=
  ∃ a, a ∉ s.cells ∧ t.cells = insert a s.cells

/-- Paper pp.24–26: an entire physical path, with inadmissible vertices excluded
at every level. The root and each edge are constructed combinatorially. -/
structure PhysicalPath (d p q : ℕ) where
  vertex : Fin (p+q+1) → Bipartition
  rootLeft : (vertex 0).1.cells = ∅
  rootRight : (vertex 0).2.cells = ∅
  admissible : ∀ k, shapeRows (vertex k).1 + shapeRows (vertex k).2 ≤ d
  edge : ∀ k : Fin (p+q),
    let a := vertex (Fin.castSucc k)
    let b := vertex (Fin.succ k)
    if k.val < p then AddsCell a.1 b.1 ∧ a.2 = b.2
    else (AddsCell b.1 a.1 ∧ a.2 = b.2) ∨ (a.1 = b.1 ∧ AddsCell a.2 b.2)

/-- Paper pp.25–28: the three edge contents, equivalently obtained by differencing
total contents plus d times the right size (Eq.(93)); no projectors enter this definition. -/
def pathContent {d p q : ℕ} (T : PhysicalPath d p q) (k : Fin (p+q)) : ℤ :=
  let a := T.vertex (Fin.castSucc k)
  let b := T.vertex (Fin.succ k)
  shapeContent b.1 - shapeContent a.1 + shapeContent b.2 - shapeContent a.2 +
    (d : ℤ) * ((b.2.cells.card : ℤ) - (a.2.cells.card : ℤ))

/-- Paper pp.13–15, Eq.(31): a walled diagram is encoded by its partially
transposed permutation. This encoding includes the empty diagram. -/
abbrev Diagram (p q : ℕ) := Equiv.Perm (Fin (p+q))

/-- Paper p.14, Eq.(31): exchange top and bottom endpoints on the dual side. -/
def flipDual (p : ℕ) {n : ℕ} (a : Bool × Fin n) : Bool × Fin n :=
  (if a.2.val < p then a.1 else !a.1, a.2)

/-- Paper pp.15,57–58: connected components after gluing opposite endpoints
in S. False denotes the top row; true denotes the bottom row. -/
def traceConnectivity {p q : ℕ} (S : Finset (Fin (p+q))) (pi : Diagram p q) :
    Setoid (Bool × Fin (p+q)) :=
  Relation.EqvGen.setoid (fun a b =>
    (∃ i, a = flipDual p (false, pi i) ∧ b = flipDual p (true, i)) ∨
    (∃ i ∈ S, a = (false,i) ∧ b = (true,i)))

/-- Paper p.15, Eq.(43): every connected pair must have the same basis label. -/
def relationMatrix {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] (d : ℕ) (R : Setoid (Bool × ι)) :
    Matrix (ι → Fin d) (ι → Fin d) ℂ := by
  classical
  exact fun x y => if ∀ a b, R.r a b →
    (if a.1 then y a.2 else x a.2) = (if b.1 then y b.2 else x b.2) then 1 else 0

/-- Paper p.15, Eqs.(41)–(43): physical action of a single walled diagram. -/
def diagramMatrix (d : ℕ) {p q : ℕ} (pi : Diagram p q) :
    Matrix (Sites (Fin d) (p+q)) (Sites (Fin d) (p+q)) ℂ :=
  relationMatrix d (traceConnectivity ∅ pi)

/-- Paper pp.13,15: the vector space of formal complex diagram combinations;
no multiplication on this vector space is needed for the trace statements. -/
abbrev DiagramSum (p q : ℕ) := Diagram p q →₀ ℂ

/-- Paper p.15, Eq.(41), extended complex-linearly to all diagrams. -/
def diagramAction (d : ℕ) {p q : ℕ} (z : DiagramSum p q) :
    Matrix (Sites (Fin d) (p+q)) (Sites (Fin d) (p+q)) ℂ :=
  z.sum (fun pi c => c • diagramMatrix d pi)

/-- Paper p.15, Eq.(38): closed components are exactly those containing no
untraced endpoint. Count components of the finite graph, independently of matrix traces. -/
def closedLoops {p q : ℕ} (S : Finset (Fin (p+q))) (pi : Diagram p q) : ℕ :=
  Nat.card {C : Quotient (traceConnectivity S pi) //
    ∀ a : Bool × Fin (p+q), Quotient.mk (traceConnectivity S pi) a = C → a.2 ∈ S}

/-- Paper p.15, Eq.(37): diagram trace is d to the number of closed loops,
extended linearly, with empty closure value d^0=1. -/
def diagramTrace (d : ℕ) {p q : ℕ} (z : DiagramSum p q) : ℂ :=
  z.sum (fun pi c => c * (d : ℂ) ^ closedLoops Finset.univ pi)

/-- Paper p.15, Eq.(38): remaining wires keep their original order on each side
of the wall. Subtype indices avoid an arbitrary renumbering. -/
abbrev Remaining {n : ℕ} (S : Finset (Fin n)) := {i : Fin n // i ∉ S}

/-- Paper p.15, Eq.(38): the contracted diagram is the connectivity relation
induced on retained endpoints after gluing in S and erasing closed components. -/
def contractedRelation {p q : ℕ} (S : Finset (Fin (p+q))) (pi : Diagram p q) :
    Setoid (Bool × Remaining S) :=
  Setoid.comap (fun a => (a.1,a.2.val)) (traceConnectivity S pi)

/-- Paper p.15, Eq.(38): diagrammatic partial trace; relations encode the
remaining matching, so parallel terms are combined as a formal linear combination. -/
def diagramPartialTrace (d : ℕ) {p q : ℕ} (S : Finset (Fin (p+q)))
    (z : DiagramSum p q) : Setoid (Bool × Remaining S) →₀ ℂ := by
  classical
  exact z.sum (fun pi c => Finsupp.single (contractedRelation S pi)
    (c * (d : ℂ) ^ closedLoops S pi))

/-- Paper p.15, Eq.(43): action of a formal combination of retained diagram relations. -/
def relationAction {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] (d : ℕ)
    (z : Setoid (Bool × ι) →₀ ℂ) : Matrix (ι → Fin d) (ι → Fin d) ℂ :=
  z.sum (fun R c => c • relationMatrix d R)

/-- Paper p.58, Eq.(188): assemble fixed retained labels and summed traced labels. -/
def joinLabels {d n : ℕ} {S : Finset (Fin n)}
    (x : Remaining S → Fin d) (z : {i : Fin n // i ∈ S} → Fin d) : (Sites (Fin d) n) :=
  fun i => if h : i ∈ S then z ⟨i,h⟩ else x ⟨i,h⟩

/-- Paper pp.57–58, Eq.(188): standard partial matrix trace on arbitrary subsets. -/
def matrixPartialTrace {d n : ℕ} (S : Finset (Fin n))
    (A : Matrix (Sites (Fin d) n) (Sites (Fin d) n) ℂ) :
    Matrix (Remaining S → Fin d) (Remaining S → Fin d) ℂ :=
  fun x y => ∑ z : {i : Fin n // i ∈ S} → Fin d, A (joinLabels x z) (joinLabels y z)

example (d : ℕ) : diagramAction d (0 : DiagramSum 0 0) = 0 := by
  simp [diagramAction]

example {d n : ℕ} {S : Finset (Fin n)} (x : Remaining S → Fin d)
    (z : {i : Fin n // i ∈ S} → Fin d) (i : Remaining S) :
    joinLabels x z i.val = x i := by simp [joinLabels, i.property]

/-- Paper pp.38–39, specialized to d=k=2: after sorting the two eigenvalues,
the largest eigenvalue t has density 6(2t-1)^2 on [1/2,1]. This follows directly
from the normalized density in Eq.(147), where k=d and no singular strata occur. -/
def qubitEigenvalueDensity (t : ℝ) : ℝ := 6 * (2*t-1)^2

/-- Paper p.38: the state with eigenvalues t,1-t and unknown eigenbasis U. -/
def qubitSpectralState (t : ℝ) (U : Matrix.unitaryGroup (Fin 2) ℂ) :
    Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ :=
  conjSuperop (U : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ)
    (Matrix.diagonal (![ (t : ℂ), ((1-t : ℝ) : ℂ)]))

/-- Paper pp.38–39, Eqs.(150),(151), source-proof reading: one fixed effect P
is chosen before the unknown U; the integral averages only the induced eigenvalue law.
The strict lower branch and weak upper branch partition the threshold event. -/
def qubitThresholdSuccess (n : ℕ) (c : ℝ)
    (P : Matrix (Sites (Fin 2) n) (Sites (Fin 2) n) ℂ)
    (U : Matrix.unitaryGroup (Fin 2) ℂ) : ℝ :=
  ∫ t in (1/2 : ℝ)..1, qubitEigenvalueDensity t *
    (Matrix.trace (piKron n (qubitSpectralState t U) *
      (if t < c then P else 1-P))).re

/-- Paper pp.38–39: achievable worst-case guarantees of a two-outcome POVM.
This avoids taking a real infimum or supremum on an unchecked empty set. -/
def qubitThresholdGuarantees (n : ℕ) (c : ℝ) : Set ℝ :=
  {F | ∃ P : Matrix (Sites (Fin 2) n) (Sites (Fin 2) n) ℂ,
    P.PosSemidef ∧ (1-P).PosSemidef ∧
      ∀ U : Matrix.unitaryGroup (Fin 2) ℂ, F ≤ qubitThresholdSuccess n c P U}

/-- Paper p.39: the polynomial defining the first breakpoint. -/
def firstBreakpointPolynomial (x : ℝ) : ℝ := 96*x^5-240*x^4+200*x^3-60*x^2+3

/-- Paper p.39: the polynomial defining the second breakpoint; its x^2 coefficient is -45. -/
def secondBreakpointPolynomial (x : ℝ) : ℝ := 24*x^5-60*x^4+70*x^3-45*x^2+15*x-3

/-- Paper p.39: isolate the first root rather than equating it to a rounded decimal.
The theorem below establishes membership and uniqueness, so the empty-set default is excluded. -/
def firstBreakpoint : ℝ :=
  sInf {x : ℝ | x ∈ Set.Ioo (8215/10000 : ℝ) (8216/10000 : ℝ) ∧
    firstBreakpointPolynomial x = 0}

/-- Paper p.39: isolate the second root; membership and uniqueness are part of the result. -/
def secondBreakpoint : ℝ :=
  sInf {x : ℝ | x ∈ Set.Ioo (9138/10000 : ℝ) (9139/10000 : ℝ) ∧
    secondBreakpointPolynomial x = 0}

/-- Paper p.39, Eq.(152), visually checked: the middle coefficient is 6/5. -/
def threeCopySuccessCurve (c : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  if c ≤ firstBreakpoint then 2*(1-c)*(4*c^2-2*c+1)
  else if c ≤ secondBreakpoint then 7/5-(6/5)*c*(16*c^4-40*c^3+40*c^2-20*c+5)
  else (2*c-1)^3

/-- Paper p.39, Eq.(153): the one-copy lower bound. -/
def oneCopySuccessCurve (c : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  max (2*(1-c)*(4*c^2-2*c+1)) ((2*c-1)^3)

example : qubitEigenvalueDensity (1/2) = 0 := by norm_num [qubitEigenvalueDensity]

/-- Paper pp.39–41, Eqs.(154),(156): frequency of one symbol in three inputs. -/
def frequency {d : ℕ} (x : (Sites (Fin d) 3)) (a : Fin d) : ℕ :=
  (Finset.univ.filter (fun i => x i = a)).card

/-- Paper pp.40–41: fidelity uses the projector onto ALL most frequent symbols,
without normalizing that projector to a mixture. -/
def majoritySuccess {d : ℕ} (Phi : Superop (Sites (Fin d) 3) (Sites (Fin d) 1))
    (x : (Sites (Fin d) 3)) : ℝ :=
  ∑ a : Fin d, if ∀ b : Fin d, frequency x b ≤ frequency x a then
    (Phi (Matrix.single x x 1) (fun _ => a) (fun _ => a)).re else 0

/-- Paper p.42, Eq.(159): physical asymmetric cloning region. All real shrinkage
parameters, including negative ones, are permitted; the same channel works on every state. -/
def RealizableCloning (d : ℕ) (r : Fin 3 → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ Phi : Superop (Fin d) (Sites (Fin d) 3), IsChannel Phi ∧
    ∀ rho, IsDensityMatrix rho → ∀ k : Fin 3,
      traceRight ((Phi rho).submatrix
        (Fin.insertNthEquiv (fun _ : Fin 3 => Fin d) k)
        (Fin.insertNthEquiv (fun _ : Fin 3 => Fin d) k)) = (r k : ℂ) • rho + (((1-r k)/(d : ℝ) : ℝ) : ℂ) • 1

/-- Paper p.42, Eqs.(162)–(164): the qubit affine map. A is the scalar block,
B the size-two block, and C the size-three block; entries are zero-based. -/
def qubitCloneCoordinates (A : ℝ) (B : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ)
    (C : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) ℂ) : Fin 3 → ℝ :=
  ![(3*(C 0 0).re + (B 0 0).re + 5*A + 3*(C 1 1).re + 9*(C 2 2).re +
      3*(B 1 1).re - 3)/3,
    (6*(C 0 0).re + 5*(B 0 0).re + 10*A + 15*(C 1 1).re +
      3*Real.sqrt 3*((C 1 2).re+(C 2 1).re) + 9*(C 2 2).re +
      Real.sqrt 3*((B 0 1).re+(B 1 0).re) + 3*(B 1 1).re - 6)/6,
    (14*(C 0 0).re + 5*(B 0 0).re + 10*A +
      2*Real.sqrt 2*((C 0 1).re+(C 1 0).re) + 7*(C 1 1).re + 9*(C 2 2).re +
      2*Real.sqrt 6*((C 0 2).re+(C 2 0).re) +
      Real.sqrt 3*((C 1 2).re+(C 2 1).re) -
      Real.sqrt 3*((B 0 1).re+(B 1 0).re) + 3*(B 1 1).re - 6)/6]

/-- Paper p.44, Eqs.(167)–(169): qutrit affine coordinates. A,B,C,D correspond
to the source's blocks X^1,X^2,X^3,X^4 with sizes 1,2,3,3. -/
def qutritCloneCoordinates (A : ℝ) (B : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ)
    (C D : Matrix (Fin 3) (Fin 3) ℂ) : Fin 3 → ℝ :=
  ![(3*(D 0 0).re + 6*(C 0 0).re + 15*(B 0 0).re + 24*A +
      3*(D 1 1).re + 12*(D 2 2).re + 6*(C 1 1).re +
      24*(C 2 2).re + 15*(B 1 1).re - 4)/8,
    (3*(D 0 0).re + 6*(C 0 0).re + 15*(B 0 0).re + 24*A +
      11*(D 1 1).re + 2*Real.sqrt 2*((D 1 2).re+(D 2 1).re) + 4*(D 2 2).re +
      22*(C 1 1).re + 4*Real.sqrt 2*((C 1 2).re+(C 2 1).re) +
      8*(C 2 2).re + 15*(B 1 1).re - 4)/8,
    (9*(D 0 0).re + 21*(C 0 0).re + 15*(B 0 0).re + 24*A +
      8*(C 2 2).re + 15*(B 1 1).re + 5*(D 1 1).re + 7*(C 1 1).re + 4*(D 2 2).re +
      2*Real.sqrt 3*((D 0 1).re+(D 1 0).re) -
      Real.sqrt 2*((D 1 2).re+(D 2 1).re) - Real.sqrt 6*((D 2 0).re+(D 0 2).re) +
      Real.sqrt 15*((C 0 1).re+(C 1 0).re) + Real.sqrt 30*((C 2 0).re+(C 0 2).re) +
      Real.sqrt 2*((C 1 2).re+(C 2 1).re) - 4)/8]

/-- Paper p.44, §7.4: a four-slot deterministic sequential protocol. A single
finite memory dimension suffices by padding the intermediate memories. All five
channels are part of the protocol, hence independent of the unknown U. -/
structure FourUseProtocol where
  memory : ℕ
  memoryPositive : 0 < memory
  prepare : Superop (Fin 2) (Fin memory × Fin 2)
  between : Fin 3 → Superop (Fin memory × Fin 2) (Fin memory × Fin 2)
  finish : Superop (Fin memory × Fin 2) (Fin 2)
  prepareChannel : IsChannel prepare
  betweenChannel : ∀ i, IsChannel (between i)
  finishChannel : IsChannel finish

/-- Paper p.44: four applications of the same unknown qubit unitary, on the
query register only, with the identity on memory. No controlled oracle is supplied. -/
def FourUseProtocol.run (P : FourUseProtocol) (U : Matrix.unitaryGroup (Fin 2) ℂ) :
    Superop (Fin 2) (Fin 2) :=
  let O := idTensorSuperop (R := Fin P.memory) (conjSuperop (U : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℂ))
  fun rho => P.finish (O (P.between 2 (O (P.between 1
    (O (P.between 0 (O (P.prepare rho))))))))

example : contraction (d := 2) (n := 2) 0 1 ![0,0] ![1,1] = 1 := by
  simp [contraction]

example : qubitCloneCoordinates (2/5) 0 0 0 = -1/3 := by
  norm_num [qubitCloneCoordinates]

example : qutritCloneCoordinates (1/8) 0 0 0 0 = -1/8 := by
  norm_num [qutritCloneCoordinates]

example (d : ℕ) (x y : (Sites (Fin d) 0)) :
    diagramMatrix d (1 : Diagram 0 0) x y = 1 := by
  classical
  simp [diagramMatrix, relationMatrix]

end

